# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tmap tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] length[8] prot[4]
size[4] Rmap tag[2] offset[8] length[8]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Tmap` is 9Px's own, with no counterpart in 9P2000 or 9P2000.L, and belongs to the `map` extension, which `Tversion` negotiates as `+map` (see version(5)).
It asks the server for the bytes of a file from <offset> for <length> as a VMO, a memory object the client maps into its address space (see vmo(2)), so that a program's `mmap` of a file is the file's own pages, not a copy.
Its type numbers are 158 and 159.

<fid> must be open, on a file that is not a directory, and open for reading: `OREAD`, `ORDWR` or `OEXEC`, but not `OWRITE`.
<prot> is the access the mapping wants: read 1, write 2, exec 4.
A writable mapping needs <fid> open `ORDWR`, as POSIX's `mmap` asks of a descriptor.
Write and exec together are refused, as are other bits and a <length> of 0; <prot> of 0 is taken, and gives a VMO that can be read.

The VMO comes beside the reply, not in it.
On the ring transport the server puts it in one of the ring's handle slots (see ring_xfer_handles(2)), and the completion carries `P9_CQE_HANDLE` in its flags and the slot's number in `aux`; the client takes it from there.
Its rights are those <prot> asks for: `VX_RIGHT_READ` and `VX_RIGHT_MAP` always, `VX_RIGHT_WRITE` or `VX_RIGHT_EXEC` as asked, and `fsd` adds `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` and `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`.
A reply that finds no free slot is sent without its VMO, which the server closes, and the client's call fails.

`Rmap`'s <offset> is where in the VMO the file's <offset> lies, and its <length> how many bytes the VMO has from there.
`fsd`'s VMO holds the whole file from its first byte, so its <offset> is the one asked for, and its <length> runs to the end of the VMO, which may be more than was asked for, or less, past the file's last page.
The client maps no more than it asked for and no more than there is, and leaves the rest of its range unmapped.
Neither offset need be a multiple of the page size, though a mapping is made of whole pages.

## The file's pages

Only a pager can make a VMO whose pages are the file's (see pager_create(2)), and only `fsd`, given `pager` in its manifest, has the extension; every other server, and `fsd` without `pager`, refuses `Tmap`.
`fsd` answers every `Tmap` of a file, from any connection and any user, with the same VMO, the file's page cache, as fsd(4) describes under {the pager|fsd(4)#pager}.
Its size is the file's in whole pages, one at least, and it follows the file's as writes change it; only the pager resizes it, never a client, so no client can shrink it under the others or grow the file with pages of zeros.
Writes through mappings reach the file: `fsd` writes the pages back every 5 seconds while files are mapped, before each commit, before a `Tread` of the file and before its size changes, and a `Twrite` goes into the pages as well as the volume, so every mapping and every reader sees one file.
In the last page, bytes past the file's end read as zeros, and what is written there is lost; past the last page nothing is mapped.
A file removed while it is mapped stays, as POSIX keeps a removed file's data while it is mapped, until nothing but `fsd` refers to the VMO.

The musl back end sends `Tmap` for an `mmap` of a file that is `MAP_SHARED`, or `MAP_PRIVATE` and not writable; a writable `MAP_PRIVATE` mapping, or one on a server without `map`, is read into a VMO of the back end's own with `Tread`.

## Errors

Before `map` is negotiated, and from a server with no `map` operation, `Tmap` is answered `operation not supported`; `fsd` without `pager` answers the same.
A <fid> that is not one of the connection's is `unknown fid`.
A <fid> not open, open on a directory, open `OWRITE`, or open for less than `ORDWR` with write asked for, is `permission denied`.
Bad <prot> bits, write with exec, and a <length> of 0 are `bad message`; an <offset> and <length> whose sum overflows 64 bits, and in `fsd` an <offset> at or past the file's last page, are `offset out of range`.
`fsd` also answers `permission denied` for its made-up files (`adm`'s `ctl` and `status`) and for a writable mapping in a read-only tree, and `out of memory` for a 65th mapped file.
VectraOS's client sends `Tmap` only once the session has `map`, failing with `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` otherwise, and fails with `VX_ERR_INVALID` an `Rmap` that comes without its VMO.

